---
/**
 * Shipping AI, Not Designing Around It — the flagship case study.
 *
 * Content from handover/PORTFOLIO-CONTENT-AND-PROMPT.md Part 1, using the
 * FACT-CHECKED copy in that file rather than the earlier draft. Two claims
 * differ between the two versions and the corrected ones are used here:
 *   - "Over a year" at Bibel TV, not "over eighteen months" (mid-Aug 2025 to
 *     now is ~12 months elapsed, ~15 over the full contract).
 *   - The campaign generator's web prototype is INTERNAL. Only the skill's
 *     outputs reach customer surfaces. Nothing here says otherwise.
 *
 * The /for/ VARIANT paragraph in the handover is deliberately NOT used: it is
 * scoped to application pages, and this is treppmann.design.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SummaryBand from '../../components/SummaryBand.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Shipping AI, Not Designing Around It"
  description="A support copilot in daily use, a campaign generation tool built end to end, and a prototyping practice engineering builds from directly."
  showBackButton={true}
>
  <section class="case-study-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="case-study-meta">AI Product &amp; Practice</p>
      <h1>Shipping AI, Not Designing Around It</h1>
      <p class="hero-tagline">Bibel TV</p>
      <p class="hero-description">
        Most design leaders in 2026 can talk about AI. Over a year at a
        broadcaster mid linear-to-streaming transformation, I moved from
        designing <em>for</em> the model to shipping production AI myself &mdash;
        a support copilot in daily use, a campaign generation tool built end to
        end, and a prototyping practice engineering now builds from directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SummaryBand
    figures={[
      { value: "~70%", label: "Of AI-generated code ships" },
      { value: "Hours", label: "Iteration, down from days" },
      { value: "4", label: "Platforms" }
    ]}
    meta="Head of Product Design · Bibel TV · agentic engineering practice"
  />

  <section class="case-study-content">
    <div class="container content content--hanging">
      <section class="cs-section">
        <h2 class="cs-section__heading">The support copilot</h2>
        <div class="cs-section__body">
      <p>
        The viewer-services team answers letters, emails, and social messages
        &mdash; often long, often emotional, sometimes in crisis. I built them a
        copilot: paste in the message, get a draft grounded in the service
        handbook &mdash; its wording, its formatting, its standard phrases.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three decisions defined it. Restricted-topic rules are enforced
        <em>in the output</em>, not left to the agent to remember. Crisis
        messages don't get drafted at all &mdash; they escalate to a person. And
        every send decision stays human; the tool never sends.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rollout was designed as carefully as the tool. I tested it on myself
        first, then ran one-on-ones &mdash; no group demo, no mandate. It stayed
        fully optional, with the team lead asking only that people try it and
        share feedback. The tool then earned its place on the hard cases rather
        than the easy ones: one of the most sceptical senior agents tried it on a
        letter that would normally cost hours &mdash; multiple parallel topics,
        strongly argued criticism &mdash; and shipped a good response in minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where it landed:</strong> complex multi-topic letters answered in
        minutes instead of hours. Temporary staff write in the company's voice
        from day one. The sceptics use it daily and told me so unprompted.
      </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section">
        <h2 class="cs-section__heading">The companion campaign generator</h2>
        <div class="cs-section__body">
      <p>
        Bibel TV runs bi-monthly fundraising campaigns, and each one needs
        supporting material across several formats &mdash; in-app notifications,
        web banners, short video scripts &mdash; all carrying the same message in
        the same voice, produced under time pressure by people who aren't
        copywriters.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built a web application that generates them. The interesting part isn't
        the generation; it's what the generation reasons from. The organisation's
        formatting guidelines, its accumulated fundraising knowledge &mdash; much
        of it previously held in people's heads rather than written down &mdash;
        and its house voice became the system's context. The interaction is a
        multi-turn clarifying-question loop rather than single-shot prompting:
        the tool asks what it needs to know about the campaign before producing
        anything, which is what makes the output usable rather than merely fast.
      </p>
      <p>
        Analysis, prompt architecture, prototype, production skill &mdash; no
        engineering hand-off at any stage.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="pull-statement">
        The institutional knowledge was always the asset. AI just made it
        addressable.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        That's the pattern across both systems. Neither tool is clever on its
        own. Each works because the organisation's own knowledge &mdash; the
        service handbook, the fundraising guidelines, the things people knew but
        had never written down &mdash; became the context the model reasons from.
      </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section">
        <h2 class="cs-section__heading">Agentic engineering as design practice</h2>
        <div class="cs-section__body">
      <p>
        I stopped shipping specs. The new experience was designed first as a
        working web prototype, then as an Android prototype, both built with
        AI-assisted coding against real production APIs. Engineering now consumes
        that prototype code directly &mdash; roughly 70% of it reaches production
        with minor modification, and iteration collapsed from days through
        spec-and-handoff to hours in working code.
      </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section">
        <h2 class="cs-section__heading">The design system as an API</h2>
        <div class="cs-section__body">
      <p>
        One token repository &mdash; DTCG standard, three tiers from global
        primitives through semantic to component, built with Style Dictionary,
        synced live to Figma and Android with iOS and web ready, architected to
        support server-driven UI. One commit propagates to every platform.
        Consistency enforced by structure rather than coordination.
      </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section">
        <h2 class="cs-section__heading">What made it work</h2>
        <div class="cs-section__body">
      <p class="closing-note">
        Nobody was told to use any of this. The copilot went out optional, with
        the team lead asking people to try it rather than requiring it. I tested
        it on myself before anyone else saw it. It reached the team on hard cases
        first: the most sceptical agent's worst letter, not a tidy demo. That
        sequence is slower than a rollout plan, and it is the part I would
        repeat.
      </p>
        </div>
      </section>
</div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Pull statement — Gambarino italic, breaking the measure deliberately
     (case-study design spec §2). Hanging opening quote, per §5. */
  .pull-statement {
    font-family: "Gambarino", Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 2rem;
    line-height: 1.28;
    margin: 3.5rem 0 3.5rem -1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
    color: var(--color-text);
    max-width: 22ch;
  }

  /* The literal glyph, not an escape. This was `"\\201C"`, which CSS reads as
     an escaped backslash followed by the text 201C — so the page rendered a
     visible \201C in front of the quote. `\201C` would also work; the character
     itself cannot be got wrong. */
  .pull-statement::before {
    content: "“";
    margin-left: -0.5em;
  }

  /* Closing note — the honest limitation, indented and italic per §3. */
  .closing-note {
    font-style: italic;
    margin-left: 2rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .pull-statement {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
      margin-left: 0;
      max-width: none;
    }
    .pull-statement::before {
      margin-left: 0;
    }
    .closing-note {
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
